Yes. The distinction is between explicit reasoning and compiled action.

If a skill has been learned deeply enough, the body can execute faster than conscious narration:

\text{perception}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern match}
\rightarrow
\text{action}

instead of:

\text{perception}
\rightarrow
\text{verbal analysis}
\rightarrow
\text{decision}
\rightarrow
\text{action}

That is what expert automaticity looks like. The catch is that reflexes are only trustworthy inside the environments that shaped and calibrated them.

So the real question is not “Can I trust my gut?” It is:

\text{Has this response been tested against reality often enough to earn trust?}

A useful calibration loop is:

\text{act}
\rightarrow
\text{observe outcome}
\rightarrow
\text{compare with expectation}
\rightarrow
\text{update}
\rightarrow
\text{repeat}

Over time, successful patterns can move from conscious calculation into automatic execution. That is almost like compilation:

\text{deliberative model}
\rightarrow
\text{repeated validation}
\rightarrow
\text{compressed policy}
\rightarrow
\text{reflex}

But you still keep the higher layer available for unfamiliar conditions. A boxer does not verbally calculate every punch, but if the opponent starts doing something novel, conscious analysis may need to re-enter the loop.

So I would frame it as:

Reason to build the reflex. Test to trust the reflex. Reopen reasoning when the environment changes.

The mind does not need to narrate every correct action. Its job may be to train, audit, and occasionally override the embodied policy.